queries.long:
Returns `1` when any of these is true, otherwise `0`:

1. the entity is in water - the same test as `query.is_in_water`;
2. rain is falling on the block at the top of the entity's hitbox - the same test as `query.is_in_water_or_rain`;
3. the entity was hit by a splash water bottle very recently.

The third case is a short window: a splash starts a counter that runs out after about one game tick, so the query reports it for a single tick at most. The splash is handled by the server, so that part does not show up in resource-pack Molang: a zombie hit by a splash water bottle read `0` on the client. Cases 1 and 2 work on both sides; see `query.is_in_water_or_rain` for where the rain test is made.

Without an entity it returns `0`.

Returns `1` when the entity's tamed flag is set and `0` otherwise.

The flag is turned on in four ways:

- a player tames the entity through `minecraft:tameable` (feeding a wolf, cat or parrot);
- a player tames a rideable mob through `minecraft:tamemount` (breaking in a horse, donkey, mule or llama);
- the entity gains the `minecraft:is_tamed` component, for example from a component group;
- a baby is born to tamed parents whose breeding settings pass the tamed state on.

Removing `minecraft:is_tamed` turns the flag off again. The flag belongs to the entity's synchronised state, so behavior-pack and resource-pack Molang read the same value, which is why vanilla uses it to pick the collar or tamed texture of wolves and cats.

The query does not look at the owner: whether the owner is online, or whether an owner is recorded at all, is answered by `query.has_owner` and `query.owner_identifier`. Without an entity it returns `0`.

Returns the time since the entity's most recent vibration detection, in seconds: the number of game ticks since the detection divided by 20, so it moves in steps of `0.05`. It is `0` on the tick of a detection and grows until the next one.

The detection time is kept by the resource-pack side of the entity (the warden uses it to pulse its tendrils). It returns `-1` when:

- the entity has no record of vibrations at all (most entities), or has not detected one yet;
- it is evaluated in behavior-pack Molang - this also writes an error to the content log;
- there is no entity - also with a content-log error.

Because `-1` is below every real value, vanilla checks `< 0` before using the number.
